    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 246,
        "text": "Welcome back to Science Weekly. I'm Maya Chen. Today we're talking about how scientists disagree—and how they communicate that disagreement to the public. With me is Dr. Leo Park, a science communication researcher. Leo, thanks for joining.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "d7d7c493144ce4c5c696a48c249d6a1f2c463822d25643959c9597b092cbfd4c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 252,
        "sourceEnd": 283,
        "text": "Thanks, Maya. Happy to be here.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "a16420afd8f2c660b81f182004b7a2598d2cd14dd48bed7985291107d874e997",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 290,
        "sourceEnd": 381,
        "text": "So, first, what does scientific disagreement actually look like? Is it like a big argument?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b10b2ef70b0d9e31e2273997f8a6e0f6908e60bdb499f3e679b6c5f9c0c0fca6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 387,
        "sourceEnd": 688,
        "text": "Not usually. Most disagreement happens in papers, at conferences, and in peer review. It's formal and slow. For example, one team publishes a result, and another team tries to repeat it. If the second team gets a different result, they write a comment. Then the first team replies. It can take months.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "4f34075f0067f121e12757fbd25378a1b3a44cee291a5eac5f8db3372e93c9f0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 695,
        "sourceEnd": 715,
        "text": "Months? That's slow.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "292cc5f078347f77977fea193d98e01ee50bf35b3646b61144d0076374269dfb",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 721,
        "sourceEnd": 818,
        "text": "Yes, but that's the point. The process is designed to be careful. It's not a fight; it's a check.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "3c31f51e01963f7c1c39c1fa4066b18e6a0f418c6bbd779bdc5a02de10ed0cf9",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 825,
        "sourceEnd": 870,
        "text": "But the public often sees it as a fight. Why?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "0123be8580c259cc20ff0033a50c1513c2aab98da66f52602256ae3ca86036ef",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 876,
        "sourceEnd": 1120,
        "text": "Because the media likes conflict. A headline like \"Scientists disagree!\" gets clicks. But the actual disagreement is often narrow. Two groups might agree on 90 percent of the data and disagree on one small point. The public sees the 10 percent.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "d70fa479dc6ecd85cc5ea374893ea10ed11def855ccb47d9a23e66a23c3f46f0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1127,
        "sourceEnd": 1189,
        "text": "So how should scientists talk about this? What's the best way?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "f4820e071eb137afcfd17d6a9bf9215f5558e3eb7cfccd7be7df2c22bf37f47c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1195,
        "sourceEnd": 1467,
        "text": "First, they should be clear about what is known and what is uncertain. Second, they should explain the process. Say: \"This is how we check results.\" Third, they should avoid saying \"the science is settled\" when it isn't. That phrase makes people distrust scientists later.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "baabc67f4a1151b1f58dd1b225d6fa3f46714d314087fe449a0f2d23190d8db7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1474,
        "sourceEnd": 1556,
        "text": "Interesting. Can you give an example of a disagreement that was communicated well?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "31a5f4ab01468482572da0e9b0e907475b25ce9c3f1e2b6598d36b04890c3ddc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1562,
        "sourceEnd": 1884,
        "text": "Sure. A few years ago, two teams disagreed about the age of a rock formation. One team said 200 million years; the other said 210 million. They published a joint statement. They said: \"We agree on the method. We disagree on one measurement. We are working together to resolve it.\" The public understood. Trust stayed high.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "3006766f5cba734abfd97b7e10639113486520472dd7c32371689da8ff7da863",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1891,
        "sourceEnd": 1937,
        "text": "That's a good model. What about a bad example?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "120853f8b29820f445066d5b621b1135ebe9fd9333d186eae1a9627ce79e7e31",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1943,
        "sourceEnd": 2131,
        "text": "A bad example is when scientists attack each other personally on social media. That makes the public think the whole field is unreliable. It's better to criticize the idea, not the person.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "3674d6f3bd6356b0a21227d6b1f669f6c46536270c69b5d030a08b98fe992ba8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2138,
        "sourceEnd": 2193,
        "text": "So the rule is: disagree with the idea, not the person?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "f19b3914665f3c0b934fc5e0eb1095606ef56f105b564bb2f4bdad052196f1d5",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 2199,
        "sourceEnd": 2313,
        "text": "Exactly. And always explain the evidence. If you say \"my colleague is wrong,\" that's not enough. You must say why.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "0964b80039163e209a2a00c51f3cbec784c15accb9419b2e5cd203735ce18bea",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2320,
        "sourceEnd": 2417,
        "text": "What about uncertainty? Scientists often say \"more research is needed.\" Does that confuse people?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "7cb481309b181cfcf5264ad720bc1956e5b9f79ae2286318355a7bb38a9b5e88",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 2423,
        "sourceEnd": 2650,
        "text": "It can. But it's honest. The problem is when scientists say it without context. A better sentence is: \"We are 80 percent confident in this result, and we need one more study to be sure.\" That gives people a number and a reason.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "11fed0632d799573df01e2daff858329e36a06bc810fb89e5086901968b63342",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2657,
        "sourceEnd": 2673,
        "text": "So numbers help?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "82d6240588d19193ac275ae7e9b659c8ab234c48beac9398c7b2f16d8fb20547",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 2679,
        "sourceEnd": 2820,
        "text": "Numbers help when they are explained. A number alone can scare people. \"A 10 percent risk\" sounds big. But \"10 out of 100 people\" is clearer.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "26e6570f9f82d03eb7d59de76db2b79b672e627a81a10258184c9817b4ac351f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2827,
        "sourceEnd": 2888,
        "text": "Let's talk about the order of information. Does order matter?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b2f71f5923892f56ab5772bb05404338045822009b9fc8bd441df303e9926b34",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 2894,
        "sourceEnd": 3065,
        "text": "Yes. Start with what you agree on. Then state the disagreement. Then explain the next step. That order builds trust. If you start with the conflict, people stop listening.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "abf3253d7846b7b9bfc507837b6730a77d22aa68bee1446b627cb5d7a980d575",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3072,
        "sourceEnd": 3111,
        "text": "So: agreement, disagreement, next step.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "3f3fd396709ad6d6d87d18d31a354812bbc1ffc131f4129036b129243d1e9093",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 3117,
        "sourceEnd": 3221,
        "text": "Exactly. And use clear linking words: \"however,\" \"therefore,\" \"in addition.\" These words show the logic.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "30b0e649f223194a0c5fe9398bebe557635173d100e81dde5fee6867436248b9",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3228,
        "sourceEnd": 3298,
        "text": "What about place? Does location matter—where the disagreement happens?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "2d4e7e0ade764fcdb04bcfa767d98334d8e51db07d41c0656a4bcaa15f8c04e2",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 3304,
        "sourceEnd": 3494,
        "text": "It can. A disagreement in a private email is different from one in a public journal. Public disagreements are seen by everyone, so they must be more careful. Private ones can be more direct.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "d719c5ed03498473fa09ed950dcf2d4ff49c3f13f29236fa5452dbfd5f2d39c6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3501,
        "sourceEnd": 3548,
        "text": "And change over time? How do disagreements end?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "3427c34dbd6b5a911d804802039606b78b975c59bce5bd7f7319334f129903f4",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 3554,
        "sourceEnd": 3794,
        "text": "They end when new data arrives. Sometimes one side changes its mind. That's a success, not a failure. But the public often sees a change of mind as weakness. We need to reframe that: changing your mind when evidence changes is good science.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "40a9091923c914416090e539fcb067cf5dbebc84fd4826a94d8fe721542b6bf4",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3801,
        "sourceEnd": 3866,
        "text": "So the message is: disagreement is normal, and it can be healthy.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "06278d42d75ce03dc67dfdfa92e5a4d68cc1053fdc00b1999f82ecbf74946e27",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 3872,
        "sourceEnd": 4009,
        "text": "Yes. The goal is not to hide disagreement. The goal is to explain it clearly. If the public sees the process, they trust the result more.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "2774dc53900e93215ef1cb648826d7d8944a9c00db30d0d584f642f7515fc448",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 4016,
        "sourceEnd": 4052,
        "text": "Leo, thank you. That was very clear.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "7efcf5a0f992ffe708bbe0ea32130494ceee5ecffc232d26c4cecd3dacba2121",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 4058,
        "sourceEnd": 4074,
        "text": "Thank you, Maya.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "c1f96f71b12e906e16e1389356068c4e000b7b89127f0362432dd46fe0044499",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
